      content: `<div class="blog-table-box"><h3>Match Your Recipient's Situation to the Best Method</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Your Recipient...</th><th>Best Method</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Has a Mexican bank account and knows their CLABE</strong></td><td>App + SPEI deposit</td><td>Cheapest and fastest combination — minutes, near-zero markup</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Has a bank account but doesn't know their CLABE</strong></td><td>Direct-to-debit-card deposit</td><td>Only needs a 16-digit card number, still low-cost and fast</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Doesn't have a bank account</strong></td><td>Cash pickup (OXXO, <a href="/companies/ria">Ria</a>, <a href="/companies/western-union">Western Union</a>, <a href="/companies/moneygram">MoneyGram</a>)</td><td>20,000+ OXXO stores plus 50,000+ agent locations nationwide</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Manages money primarily on a smartphone</strong></td><td>Mobile wallet (Mercado Pago, Spin, bank apps)</td><td>Instant notifications, spend or cash out from one app</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Needs a very large or business-related transfer</strong></td><td>Negotiated bank wire</td><td>Worth the cost only at scale, with a negotiated rate</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>For the vast majority of people sending money home to <a href="/send-money/usa-to-mexico">Mexico</a>, the answer is simple: if your recipient has any kind of bank account, ask for their CLABE and send via an app that supports SPEI. It's consistently the cheapest, fastest option — and it works around the clock, every day of the year.</p>
<p>5 Ways to Cut Your Transfer Cost Further</p>
<p>Compare total pesos received, not the advertised fee. A "$0 fee" provider can still cost you more than a competitor charging $5, once the <a href="/guides/exchange-rate-markup-explained">exchange rate markup</a> is factored in. Always check the final MXN amount on <a href="/send-money/usa-to-mexico">our comparison tool</a> before confirming.</p>
<p>Choose SPEI over cash pickup whenever possible. Direct bank deposits carry the smallest markups on this corridor — often a third to a half of what cash pickup costs for the same transfer.</p>
<p>Fund your transfer from a bank account or debit card, not a credit card. Credit card funding typically adds 1.5-3% in cash-advance-style surcharges on top of whatever the provider already charges.</p>
<p>Send larger amounts less often. Many providers charge flat fees that matter more on small transfers. Combining two $250 sends into one $500 send can cut your relative cost significantly. See our guide on <a href="/guides/cheapest-way-to-send-money-internationally">the cheapest ways to send money internationally</a> for more on this.</p>
<p>Watch the USD/MXN rate and time your transfer. The peso can move meaningfully within a single day. Set rate alerts with providers like <a href="/companies/wise">Wise</a> or <a href="/companies/remitly">Remitly</a>, and avoid sending right after major US or Mexican economic announcements when spreads tend to widen. Our <a href="/guides/exchange-rate-markup-explained">exchange rate markup guide</a> explains exactly how these spreads work.</p>
<p>Rules, Taxes, and Safety to Know Before You Send</p>
<p>A few rules and protections are worth knowing before you send money from the US to Mexico:</p>
<p>Mexico doesn't tax incoming remittances. Money sent home for family support isn't treated as taxable income for the recipient, according to <a href="https://www.banxico.org.mx/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Banco de México</a>.</p>
<p>The new US remittance excise tax mostly won't apply to you. A 1% federal tax now applies to cash-funded transfers sent through money transmitters — but transfers funded from a US bank account, debit card, or credit card are exempt. Choosing an app and funding digitally avoids it entirely. Read our <a href="/guides/us-remittance-tax-2026">full breakdown of the US remittance tax</a> for the details.</p>
<p>Large transfers get reported, not blocked. US financial institutions report transfers over $10,000 to <a href="https://www.fincen.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">FinCEN</a> under the Bank Secrecy Act. This is routine and legal — but deliberately splitting a transfer into smaller pieces to dodge the threshold ("structuring") is a federal offense.</p>
<p>Your recipient will need valid ID for cash pickup. For pickups above roughly $300, Mexican agents require a valid INE/IFE voter credential or passport. Larger monthly totals may trigger extra verification steps.</p>
<p>You're protected by US consumer rules. The <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB)</a> requires licensed providers to disclose the exact fee, exchange rate, and total amount your recipient will get — before you confirm the transfer. If a provider won't show you that number upfront, that's a red flag. See our <a href="/guides/money-transfer-safety-guide">money transfer safety guide</a> for more on spotting trustworthy providers.</p>
<p>For more context on this corridor, see our dedicated <a href="/guides/send-money-to-mexico-guide">guide to sending money to Mexico</a>, which ranks providers head-to-head with live rate data.</p>`,
