      heading: "What Are the Tax and Compliance Rules?",
      content: `<p>US sender: No tax on personal remittances. Gifts to foreign persons over $18,000 per recipient per year require filing <a href="https://www.irs.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">IRS</a> Form 709 (but no tax is typically owed). Transfers over $10,000 are reported via CTR.</p>
<p>Chinese recipient: Personal remittances from abroad are generally not subject to Chinese income tax. However, China taxes global income of tax residents — if the money is payment for services, it may be taxable.</p>
<p>FBAR requirement: If you hold financial accounts in China (including through a business) with aggregate value over $10,000 at any point in the year, you must file an FBAR (FinCEN Form 114).</p>
<p>FATCA: Chinese banks report US person account information to the IRS under FATCA agreements.</p>
<p>For more guidance, read our <a href="/guides/money-transfer-safety-guide">money transfer safety guide</a> and <a href="/guides/cheapest-way-to-send-money-internationally">cheapest international transfer guide</a>.</p>`,
